[
    {
        "content": "<p>I can use the tactic <code>apply M</code> when the conclusion of theorem M unifies with the goal I'm attempting to prove.  However, suppose that theorem M has the form ∀x (Ax ∨ Bx ∨ Cx), where Cx unifies with my goal.  In that situation <code>apply</code> won't work.  However, M can be rewritten in the logically equivalent form ∀x (¬Ax → ¬Bx → Cx).  In that form, <code>apply</code> will work!  So I think it would be nice if <code>apply</code> would do this automatically, or if there were a variant of <code>apply</code> that could do that.</p>\n<p>To put it slightly differently, I'm looking for a variant of <code>apply</code> that will work when the theorem being applied has a disjunctive form, and I'd like this <code>apply</code> to perform a step of propositional resolution between the theorem and the goal.</p>\n<p>I may try writing this tactic myself - it would be a useful exercise.  However, I don't want to reinvent the wheel.  Is there some <code>apply</code>-like tactic that can handle this case, or some easy way to combine <code>apply</code> with another tactic to achieve this?</p>\n<p>Here is an example to make this more concrete.  Consider this proof attempt:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">axiom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">axiom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∨</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>apply</code> fails:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Tactic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`apply</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">failed</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">could</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">not</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unify</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">conclusion</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">of</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`foo</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">?</span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∨</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?</span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span>\n<span class=\"k\">with</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">goal</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>As we can see in the error message, the goal unifies with one of the disjuncts, but not with the entire conclusion of <code>foo</code>.</p>\n<p>If we rewrite <code>foo</code> in an equivalent logical form, <code>apply</code> will work:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">axiom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo2</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo2</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Of course, in this trivial example the rewritten <code>foo2</code> is identical to the example we are trying to prove!  More generally, however, I think an <code>apply</code> that could handle disjunctions could be a useful tool.</p>",
        "id": 616385998,
